re risk when co-administered with MAOIs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The opioid analgesic 哌替啶 (pethidine) interacts dangerously with MAOIs, potentially causing excitation, hyperthermia, delirium, seizures, and respiratory depression. This interaction arises because MAOIs inhibit hepatic enzyme systems responsible for metabolizing pethidine, leading to elevated plasma concentrations and prolonged pharmacological effects. Clinical guidelines explicitly warn against concurrent use, recommending a minimum two-week washout period between discontinuing an MAOI and initiating pethidine therapy. This contraindication is supported by multiple sources, including pharmacological reviews and case-based evidence [Data: Entities (4489); Relationships (31306)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Compound dextromethorphan formulations are contraindicated with MAOIs due to serotonin syndrome risk\",\n            \"explanation\": \"复方氢溴酸右美沙芬 (compound dextromethorphan hydrobromide), a common ingredient in over-the-counter cold medicines, is classified as a potent serotonergic agent. When combined with MAOIs, it significantly increases the risk of serotonin syndrome—a potentially fatal condition characterized by mental status changes, autonomic instability, and neuromuscular abnormalities. Medical literature explicitly advises against co-administration, listing specific products like phenylephrine/dextromethorphan suspensions and pediatric formulations as unsafe during MAOI therapy [Data: Entities (4718); Relationships (31312)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Tyramine-rich foods trigger hypertensive crises when consumed with MAOIs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"含酪胺食物 (tyramine-containing foods) represent a major dietary hazard for patients on MAOI therapy. MAOIs block the enzymatic degradation of tyramine in the gut and liver, leading to excessive norepinephrine release and acute hypertensive episodes that may result in intracranial hemorrhage. This well-established interaction necessitates strict dietary restrictions during MAOI treatment. Clinical guidance consistently identifies aged, fermented, or spoiled protein-rich foods as primary sources of tyramine [Data: Entities (12351); Relationships (31304)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Avocado (牛油果) contains biogenic amines that interact dangerously with MAOIs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"牛油果 (avocado) is specifically flagged in the data as containing significant levels of biogenic amines, including tyramine. When consumed alongside MAOIs such as selegiline, the impaired metabolism of tyramine can lead to sudden and severe hypertension, known as a 'hypertensive crisis.' This risk underscores the importance of patient counseling on dietary restrictions beyond just aged cheeses and cured meats—commonly known triggers—and includes less obvious items like avocado [Data: Entities (12572); Relationships (43530)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Beef liver (牛肝) is a high-tyramine food requiring avoidance during MAOI therapy\",\n            \"explanation\": \"牛肝 (beef liver) is identified as a tyramine-rich food that poses a significant interaction risk with MAOIs. Organ meats, particularly when stored or aged, accumulate tyramine through bacterial decarboxylation of amino acids. Consumption of beef liver while on MAOI therapy can precipitate a hypertensive emergency. Although the supporting evidence does not specify quantitative tyramine levels, its inclusion among restricted foods in psychiatric and pharmacological literature aligns with broader dietary guidelines for MAOI users [Data: Entities (12573); Relationships (43538)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Alcoholic beverages containing tyramine are hazardous with MAOIs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"含酪胺的酒类 (tyramine-containing alcoholic beverages), such as certain red wines, tap beers, and aged liquors, are contraindicated during MAOI treatment. Fermentation processes in alcohol production can generate substantial tyramine content. Co-ingestion with MAOIs inhibits tyramine breakdown, leading to catecholamine surges and potentially life-threatening blood pressure spikes. Patients on MAOIs are routinely advised to avoid such beverages entirely [Data: Entities (12348); Relationships (32761)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Aged sausage (陈年香肠) represents a classic dietary contraindication with MAOIs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"陈年香肠 (aged sausage) is a prototypical high-tyramine food frequently cited in warnings about MAOI dietary restrictions. The aging and fermentation processes used in sausage production promote tyramine formation from tyrosine. Ingestion of aged sausage during MAOI therapy can result in acute hypertensive reactions. Its repeated mention in psychiatric and pharmacological safety literature reinforces its status as a key item to avoid [Data: Entities (12906); Relationships (53802)].\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 8.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to the life-threatening nature of multiple documented drug–drug and drug–food interactions involving MAOIs, which can cause serotonin syndrome, hypertensive crisis, or death if mismanaged.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1782120823, "model": "qwen3-max", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1579, "prompt_tokens": 6576, "total_tokens": 8155, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1280}}, "formatted_response": null, "content": "{\n    \"title\": \"MAOI Drug Interaction Network: Piperidine, Dextromethorphan, and Tyramine-Rich